# summary-honesty.manifest — what each suite's green does NOT certify,
# recorded as DATA on BOTH axes the mechanisms vary on.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE WAS REBUILT (your-org/nexus-code#821) ──────────────────
#
# The first version recorded one bucket — "hand-rolled summary with no count
# guard", 172 files — and treated everything else as protected. The `#819`
# skeptic showed that was wrong in a way that mattered, and measuring the
# correction showed it was wrong more widely than reported:
#
#   * A COUNT GUARD AND THE LEDGER PROTECT DIFFERENT THINGS, and neither
#     implies the other. Treating "routes through th_summary_and_exit" as
#     "protected" conflated them.
#   * FIVE of the 17 files excluded as "count-guarded" had NO count guard at
#     all. The classifier matched the WORD `EXPECTED` anywhere in the file —
#     including `EXPECTED_TARGET` (a config value), `EXPECTED_SLUG` (a path),
#     and, in `test-deliveries-split.sh`, the string `UNEXPECTEDLY` inside a
#     printf. It matched a substring, not a mechanism.
#   * ONE more used a FLOOR (`-lt`) rather than an exact comparison. A suite
#     can clear a floor and still under-assert, so a floor is not protection
#     against a vanished assertion; it only catches a collapse.
#
# ── THE TWO AXES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   ledger=yes   routes through `th_summary_and_exit`, so its green certifies
#                that SOMETHING was asserted and that no FAIL was swallowed by
#                a subshell (your-org/nexus-code#805).
#                It certifies NOTHING about how much was asserted.
#
#   count=exact  compares its assertion total against an expected total with
#                `!=`/`==`, so a VANISHED assertion reddens (#807).
#   count=floor  compares with `-lt`/`-gt`. Catches a collapse below the floor;
#                a suite can clear the floor and still under-assert.
#   count=subset an exact comparison, but against a NAMED SUBSET of the
#                suite's assertions (`EXPECTED_SURVIVAL_ASSERTIONS=4` over 58):
#                a vanished assertion OUTSIDE the subset reddens nothing.
#                Credited below exact on purpose (your-org/nexus-code#1365).
#   count=none   nothing pins how much ran.
#
# A file is omitted from this manifest only when it is `ledger=yes` AND
# `count=exact`. Measured on this tree: **6 of 253** banner-printing files.
# That number is the honest headline, and it is much smaller than the earlier
# "60 protected" — because that figure was counting one axis and reporting it
# as though it covered both.
#
# ── WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The classification is STRUCTURAL: which mechanisms a file carries, decidable
# from source. It does NOT claim a listed file actually under-asserts today, or
# that an omitted one is correct — proving either is a per-file argument. It
# claims only what a reader can check: this green is not backed by that
# mechanism.
#
# ── THE RATCHET ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Protection may only INCREASE. A file whose recorded state weakens reds; a
# banner-printing file missing from this list reds; a file that becomes
# `ledger=yes`+`count=exact` must have its line DELETED, or the list stops
# describing the tree. Do not regenerate this to clear a red — regenerating is
# how a measurement becomes a rubber stamp.
#
# TWO WAYS A FILE CAN BE MISSING, and they are not the same act:
#
#   YOU ARE ADDING THE SUITE. Route it through `th_summary_and_exit` and give it
#   an exact count guard. Do NOT append a line — appending is opting your own new
#   code out of the standard, which is the whole thing this file exists to stop.
#
#   IT ARRIVED BY MERGE from another change. Then the manifest is simply out of
#   date, and recording it is the honest act: this list must describe the tree,
#   and silently rewriting another author's just-landed suite to clear your own
#   red is worse than recording reality. Record it, note where it came from, and
#   the ratchet keeps the number falling from there — its author owns raising it.
#
# The distinction is between opting YOUR code out (never) and describing SOMEONE
# ELSE'S accurately (always).
#
# For a suite built on DELIBERATE failing assertions (positive controls run in
# `$( … )`), `th_expect_fail` is the sanctioned way to adopt the ledger without
# a false red. See its header in _test_helpers.sh.
#
# Checked by monitor/watcher/test-summary-honesty-manifest.sh
#
# <path>::ledger=<yes|no>::count=<exact|floor|none>
#
# THE 17 HAND-ROLLED-TALLY ROWS (your-org/nexus-code#915) are interleaved below
# in sorted position. They were NOT newly written and did NOT arrive by merge:
# they were always here and always unguarded, and the guard could not SEE them
# because its selector matched footer STRINGS while theirs read
# `passed=$PASS failed=$FAIL`. #915 removed the proxy — every tracked
# *test-*.sh is now in the population unless declared a non-suite — and these
# appeared at their true level. Recording them is not an exemption; it is the
# first time their deficiency has been on the record. Each still needs helper
# adoption plus an EXPECTED-count guard, its own author's work.
#
